# Line endings: check out LF on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so without this file a clone
# on Windows gets CRLF in the working tree.  That breaks two things:
#
#   * scripts mounted into the build containers run under a Linux bash, which
#     rejects the trailing carriage return ("\r: command not found");
#   * anything that hashes or diffs working-tree bytes (contract and bundle
#     checks, generated files) sees different bytes than the Linux/macOS CI.
#
# Storing and checking out LF everywhere keeps the bytes identical on Linux,
# macOS and Windows.  Files that must keep CRLF (batch scripts) say so below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows launchers are read by cmd.exe, so they keep the CRLF they ship with.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Binary assets and build products: never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf   binary
*.bin   binary
*.elf   binary
*.img   binary
*.hex   binary
*.o     binary
*.a     binary
*.so    binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.msi   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.tar   binary
